1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,000
Hi, if you're like me, you spend hours scrolling on TikTok or Pinterest looking at Minecraft building ideas without the intent to ever actually build them.

2
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:12,000
Now, I don't know about you, but sometimes inspiration just randomly hits.

3
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:15,000
And I have to play Minecraft. Like, that's all that's on my mind.

4
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:22,000
And lately I've been seeing a whole lot of cottagecore Minecraft content circulating around the internet, getting a lot of views.

5
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:28,000
And so I downloaded some shaders and launched Minecraft at like 6fps and built this for nostalgia.

6
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:57,980
And if you're like literally any person who's played Minecraft over the years, these houses were the bomb. They were like move-in ready. Of course, there was only room for like three things to fit in there. And then there was this problem. It's Minecraft cottagecore aesthetic because the mushroom houses with this aesthetic are like next level. They are chef's kiss. They are immaculate. And suddenly your wikiHow tutorial on how to make simple mushroom houses featuring acacia plank flooring are more an aesthetic.

7
00:00:57,980 --> 00:01:13,980
So now I present 3 ways to make mushroom houses in Minecraft!

8
00:01:13,980 --> 00:01:15,980
Yaaaaay!

9
00:01:15,980 --> 00:01:18,980
So, the first house is pretty easy.

10
00:01:18,980 --> 00:01:23,980
You're gonna wanna start out with a statistically impossible mushroom in the middle of a field,

11
00:01:23,980 --> 00:01:25,980
and use bone meal to make it big.

12
00:01:25,980 --> 00:01:31,420
Now stare at that mushroom until you decide to mutilate it to look aesthetic, like this.

13
00:01:31,420 --> 00:01:36,860
I wanted the mushroom to look a bit different than the other simple mushroom houses you typically see,

14
00:01:36,860 --> 00:01:40,940
so I went for more of a bell-shaped curve for the top of the mushroom.

15
00:01:41,660 --> 00:01:44,860
You could also use one of those mini water bottles for reference.

16
00:01:45,420 --> 00:01:50,140
I modeled mine based on an upside-down flower pot, and I think it turned out pretty good.

17
00:01:50,860 --> 00:01:55,420
Once you have achieved maximum upside-down flower pot conformity,

18
00:01:55,420 --> 00:01:59,020
Then you can start modifying the stem to make it look stalkier.

19
00:01:59,020 --> 00:02:09,020
I used the mushroom stem block and smooth court stairs, and if you're in survival, you'll probably want to mine a few of the big mushrooms with a silk touch axe to get the materials for this.

20
00:02:09,020 --> 00:02:12,020
I used birch plank flooring to match the rest of the interior.

21
00:02:12,020 --> 00:02:25,567
Now I really like this house but there not much room Sorry for my spore sense of humor Here the big grand reveal of this house Very simple I give it a 8 out of 10

22
00:02:25,567 --> 00:02:26,567
Next house.

23
00:02:26,567 --> 00:02:42,567
This was a little bit more difficult than the last one, and so you're gonna start out by making a frame that looks like the outline of a handmade cube of playdoh, the kind that the edges never seem sharp enough, and then start sculpting it to look like the body of this absurdly priced plastic Minnie Mouse teapot I found on Amazon.

24
00:02:42,567 --> 00:03:12,547
Amazon. I use smooth quartz blocks, slabs, and stairs to accomplish this. And if you want to use mushroom stem blocks, I'm sure you could, but I just like quartz because it makes the mushroom stem seem smooth and continuous, sort of like a differentiable equation. Also because it's not a pain to get all those stems. After this, start making the stem taller to look almost like a BB-8, and then give the wannabe BB-8 some helicopter blades. These will be the radius of your circular mushroom cap. Then connect those little helicopter blades.

25
00:03:12,547 --> 00:03:36,547
I never really found these graphs useful. I always find them on like tiktok. But then again, I always just wing it and it doesn't look very circular so maybe they're helpful to you, but mine turned out pretty great in the end. Anyway, start outlining the circle in red mushroom blocks and then be indecisive and then decide on making the easy way out and make a circular spherical looking mushroom cap.

26
00:03:36,547 --> 00:03:42,547
Mine is about 6 blocks tall, and unlike me, you probably want to make sure there's no weird blocks left exposed.

27
00:03:42,547 --> 00:03:48,547
Then, I made the door and the window and outlined them in the most superior wood type, spruce wood.

28
00:03:48,547 --> 00:03:52,547
I used planks, fences, and stairs as well as oak leaves for the window boxes.

29
00:03:52,547 --> 00:03:57,547
To add some extra whimsy, you can make a dilapidated cobblestone chimney and drape that with oak leaves.

30
00:03:57,547 --> 00:04:04,547
To add some stuff to the interior, I connected the chimney to a fireplace furnace type thing

31
00:04:04,547 --> 00:04:07,567
And added an unnecessary amount of bookshelves and leaves.

32
00:04:07,567 --> 00:04:10,287
Because when in doubt, add oak leaves and bookshelves.

33
00:04:10,287 --> 00:04:11,287
That's my motto.

34
00:04:11,287 --> 00:04:15,867
Also, I used flowerpots, turtle eggs, and sea pickles for the inside, and then I discovered

35
00:04:15,867 --> 00:04:21,227
that in 1.16.5, you can place turtle eggs on flowerpots, which is kind of weird.

36
00:04:21,227 --> 00:04:25,847
Anywho, I used vines as a ladder to the attic area, which will be a cute little bedroom.

37
00:04:25,847 --> 00:04:30,207
I outlined the roof with spruce wood and then placed some bookshelves behind them.

38
00:04:30,207 --> 00:04:42,853
This would also be a cool enchanting room but the house demands a bedroom So I added in a skylight which looks good with the shaders and I sure it would look good without them So this is the finished build It only took me about 45 minutes to do in creative so not too bad

39
00:04:45,333 --> 00:04:46,133
Ta-da!

40
00:04:48,133 --> 00:04:54,213
Alright, on to the last one. This one is kinda hard but not like prior gaming at the end of his TikToks kinda hard.

41
00:04:54,213 --> 00:04:59,493
You're gonna wanna start out with a circular base like such and then add a small circle attached to it.

42
00:04:59,493 --> 00:05:03,313
Once you have your two little circle buddies, start making them taller and rounder.

43
00:05:03,313 --> 00:05:07,253
I focused on the bigger circle first because it's going to be the centerpiece for the build.

44
00:05:07,253 --> 00:05:15,353
Also, if this video gets 100 likes, I'll make a video of my little sister following this tutorial to see how she does because she's bad at building and I'm bad at explaining things.

45
00:05:15,353 --> 00:05:25,253
Okay, back to the tutorial. If you don't know what shape to make the base of the big mushroom, I referred to the gourd from the 2007 Disney classic, Secret of the Magic Gourd, and mine turned out pretty alright.

46
00:05:25,253 --> 00:05:29,353
Also, I need to know, was anyone else low-key traumatized by that movie?

47
00:05:29,353 --> 00:05:33,153
Because when I first saw that movie, I guess I just thought gourds were strange pumpkins.

48
00:05:33,153 --> 00:05:36,973
So for the longest time, I was fundamentally confused on what gourds were.

49
00:05:37,973 --> 00:05:46,113
So anyway, you're gonna start making another one of those helicopter circle things and here's here's a better explanation using calculus if you want to know how to make circles in Minecraft.

50
00:05:46,973 --> 00:05:48,913
Presenting, building circles, yay!

51
00:05:49,193 --> 00:05:52,053
So you're gonna want to start out with this sort of graph paper.

52
00:05:52,053 --> 00:05:57,253
just trace something circular onto it and then fill in the blocks that the circle crosses.

53
00:05:57,253 --> 00:06:01,333
Make sure it all has the same radius. And then this is where the calculus comes in. If you look at

54
00:06:01,333 --> 00:06:07,733
tangent lines, you know what tangent lines are, I hope. You probably don't. Um, but the slope of the

55
00:06:07,733 --> 00:06:13,413
tangent line is sort of the slope of- if you were to draw the slope through the, like, the grid squares

56
00:06:13,413 --> 00:06:20,453
of the circle, this made a lot more sense inside my head. But you can trace pretty much any circle

57
00:06:20,453 --> 00:06:26,213
Just make sure the radius is the same and that each quadrant is the same.

58
00:06:26,213 --> 00:06:28,773
And there you go, you have a circle in Minecraft.

59
00:06:28,773 --> 00:06:33,293
Okay, now moving on to our other little mushroom buddy.

60
00:06:33,293 --> 00:06:36,493
You're gonna want to make him a wonky little mushroom that sort of stems off from the big

61
00:06:36,493 --> 00:06:37,493
one.

62
00:06:37,493 --> 00:06:40,893
It's really hard to explain how I did this, but I would recommend using organic shapes

63
00:06:40,893 --> 00:06:43,853
and have the top of the mushroom thinner than the base.

64
00:06:43,853 --> 00:06:45,493
Then add a tiny little cap.

65
00:06:45,493 --> 00:06:49,093
For the top of the big mushroom, I basically did the same thing as I did for the medium

66
00:06:49,093 --> 00:07:19,820
tutorial and despite it being vastly bigger the cap is about the same height Maybe even 5 blocks this time I kinda wanted to make it sort of sushi like the first build but I lost all my brain cells thinking about calculus If you want the mushroom to be super cottagecore you can add some birch leaves that hang like vines and some hanging lanterns You could also use shroom lights I also added a dilapidated chimney to mash the vibe And then for the interior I started again by connecting the fireplace to the chimney and built a loft area with a ladder leading up to it

67
00:07:19,820 --> 00:07:24,380
And then I got distracted by a wandering trader and quickly resumed my work.

68
00:07:24,380 --> 00:07:28,540
And I used an absurd amount of birch leaves to decorate.

69
00:07:28,540 --> 00:07:32,260
If you can't tell from my Pinterest page, birch leaves are my favorite.

70
00:07:32,260 --> 00:07:35,860
Also if you're wondering how they move in the wind, that's the shaders and I use BSL

71
00:07:35,860 --> 00:07:36,780
shaders.

72
00:07:36,780 --> 00:07:39,540
As far as I know, they only work for Java edition.

73
00:07:39,540 --> 00:07:43,400
I made a little kitchen space in front of one of the windows, complete with some cake and

74
00:07:43,400 --> 00:07:46,880
some turtle eggs, and then added some seating by the fire.

75
00:07:46,880 --> 00:07:52,160
In front of the other window, I made what HGTV calls a breakfast nook, complete with some plants.

76
00:07:52,160 --> 00:07:55,740
While the bedroom space is smaller than the previous build, I think it turned out nicely

77
00:07:55,740 --> 00:07:57,680
and I wouldn't mind living there.

78
00:07:57,680 --> 00:08:01,620
After decorating the bedroom, I remembered there was a bunch of empty space in the loft

79
00:08:01,620 --> 00:08:05,700
which I promptly decorated with barrels because that can be practical when they're not converting

80
00:08:05,700 --> 00:08:10,420
all the villagers to fishers, and I did notice I accidentally made a sort of shrine to Pinocchio,

81
00:08:10,420 --> 00:08:11,840
which is cool I guess.

82
00:08:11,840 --> 00:08:16,060
On the outside, I was going for a house-ish entrance.

83
00:08:16,060 --> 00:08:20,440
But I ended up making it more like a hobbit hole with spruce trapdoors and slabs and stairs.

84
00:08:20,440 --> 00:08:26,260
I also added some dark oak for better contrast and ended up expanding the windows downward

85
00:08:26,260 --> 00:08:28,420
because it just looked better.

86
00:08:28,420 --> 00:08:32,380
I added some shutters to the bedroom window and shroom lights to better illuminate it

87
00:08:32,380 --> 00:08:33,840
at night.

88
00:08:33,840 --> 00:08:37,100
There was something that was still missing though and I couldn't decide what to do so

89
00:08:37,100 --> 00:08:41,440
I tried this thing I saw on TikTok where you make mushrooms out of end rods and mushroom

90
00:08:41,440 --> 00:08:45,680
blocks which I was a bit skeptical of but I really liked how they turned out.

91
00:08:45,680 --> 00:08:57,680
So yeah, those are 3 mushroom houses you could easily build in Minecraft. Comment below which one you'd live in, I know I definitely would pick the second one because of the skylight, but that's just me.

92
00:08:57,680 --> 00:09:07,680
And if you enjoy this content and would like to see more dollar store green tutorials, please feel free to hit subscribe. See y'all later, bye!
